{
  "page": 63,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        170.1251983642578,
        39.52751922607422,
        407.9410095214844,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "331",
      "bbox": [
        428.84710693359375,
        39.52751922607422,
        443.8030700683594,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) It is computationally efﬁcient to obtain a good adaptive proposal because the cost of constructing",
      "bbox": [
        57.753814697265625,
        57.378700256347656,
        443.60308837890625,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "such a proposal is negligible compared with the cost of evaluating f.y|θ, u/ by the particle ﬁlter.",
      "bbox": [
        72.99669647216797,
        66.5881118774414,
        434.1131591796875,
        75.65313720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(c) A well-constructed proposal can be much more efﬁcient than an adaptive random-walk proposal.",
      "bbox": [
        57.75384521484375,
        77.30387115478516,
        441.34539794921875,
        85.61572265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(d) Independent Metropolis–Hastings proposals are attractive because they can be easily run in parallel,",
      "bbox": [
        57.75384521484375,
        87.2664566040039,
        443.6104431152344,
        95.57830810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "thus signiﬁcantly reducing the computation time of particle-based Bayesian inference.",
      "bbox": [
        72.9967269897461,
        97.22904205322266,
        397.17694091796875,
        105.5408935546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(e) When the particle ﬁlter is used, the marginal likelihood of any model is obtained in an efﬁcient and",
      "bbox": [
        57.75384521484375,
        107.1916275024414,
        443.6041259765625,
        115.50347900390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "unbiased manner, making model comparison straightforward.",
      "bbox": [
        72.9967269897461,
        117.15421295166016,
        308.7502136230469,
        125.466064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Miika Toivanen and Jouko Lampinen (Helsinki University of Technology, Espoo)",
      "bbox": [
        48.08985900878906,
        140.3180389404297,
        348.6023254394531,
        148.6298828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We congratulate the authors for introducing the idea of combining ‘ordinary’ Markov chain Monte Carlo",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        150.28062438964844,
        443.60308837890625,
        158.59246826171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(MCMC) and sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) methodologies in a novel way, namely using SMC algo-",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        160.2432098388672,
        443.6113586425781,
        168.5550537109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "rithms for designing proposal distributions for MCMC algorithms. We wish to share brieﬂy our own",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        170.20579528808594,
        443.609375,
        178.51763916015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "experience on using particle Monte Carlo methods on a static problem, related to computer vision.",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        180.16835021972656,
        421.94384765625,
        188.48019409179688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Consider having a few dozen feature points and a posterior distribution of their locations in a test image,",
      "bbox": [
        57.056243896484375,
        190.1309051513672,
        443.602294921875,
        198.4427490234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Owing to the combinatorial explosion, approximate methods are needed to compute the integrals that",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        200.0934600830078,
        443.60479736328125,
        208.40530395507812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "involve the posterior distribution. The multimodality of the posterior distribution complicates the approx-",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        210.05601501464844,
        443.6050109863281,
        218.36785888671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "imation problem. Although MCMC methods can be efﬁcient in exploring a single mode, the probability",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        220.01856994628906,
        443.6031494140625,
        228.33041381835938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for them to switch a mode during the sampling is low, especially if the modes are far apart. Although some",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        229.9811248779297,
        443.61297607421875,
        238.29296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "improvements to overcome this disadvantage exist, the population Monte Carlo (PMC) scheme offers a",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        239.9436798095703,
        443.6083984375,
        248.25552368164062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "much more natural approach.",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        249.90623474121094,
        160.91404724121094,
        258.21807861328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PMC techniques are based on the idea of representing the posterior with a weighted set of particles.",
      "bbox": [
        57.056243896484375,
        259.8687744140625,
        443.60308837890625,
        268.1806335449219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Each particle can be considered as a hypothesis about the correct location of the feature set and the weight",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        269.8313293457031,
        443.6047668457031,
        278.1431884765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reveals the goodness of the hypothesis. The particles are sampled from proposal distributions, which are",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        279.79388427734375,
        443.6048583984375,
        288.1057434082031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "allowed to differ between the particles and iterations. Hence, heuristics can safely be incorporated to guide",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        289.7564392089844,
        443.60223388671875,
        298.06829833984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the sampler towards the modes of the posterior, without jeopardizing the theoretical convergence issues. In",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        299.718994140625,
        443.606689453125,
        308.0308532714844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "our implementation, the proposals are Gaussian distributions, which have the previous estimate as mean",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        309.6815490722656,
        443.60931396484375,
        317.993408203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "value and whose variance decreases for particles with high posterior probability. Owing to the resampling,",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        319.64410400390625,
        443.6049499511719,
        327.9559631347656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the weakest hypotheses die, and the resulting particle set gives often a good representation of the posterior",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        329.6066589355469,
        443.6084289550781,
        337.91851806640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "distribution (Toivanen and Lampinen, 2009a,b).",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        339.5692138671875,
        231.89295959472656,
        347.8810729980469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Also SMC methods can be applied to sample the posterior, by updating the parameter vector incre-",
      "bbox": [
        57.056243896484375,
        349.5317687988281,
        443.6101379394531,
        357.8436279296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "mentally (Toivanen and Lampinen, 2009c; Tamminen and Lampinen, 2006). The previously sampled",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        359.49432373046875,
        443.608642578125,
        367.8061828613281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "components guide the sampler via the conditional prior distribution and the number of distinct modes",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        369.4568786621094,
        443.61041259765625,
        377.76873779296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "decreases as the parameter vector expands. However, because the resampling is not based on the whole",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        379.41943359375,
        443.6022033691406,
        387.7312927246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "parameter vector, unlike in PMC methods, the method is prone to lead to a particle set representing a",
      "bbox": [
        48.08984375,
        389.3819885253906,
        443.60394287109375,
        397.69384765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
